---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Print Culture and the Modern World | CBSE Class 10 History | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 History Chapter 4 revision notes on Print Culture and the Modern World. Gutenberg press, Protestant Reformation, reading mania, French Revolution, print in India, women writers, censorship, Vernacular Press Act 1878, and 20-question quiz."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Print Culture
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · History Chapter 4</span>
      <h1>Print Culture and the Modern World</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Explore how print transformed European and Indian society: from Gutenberg's movable type press to the spread of ideas, religious reform, vernacular literature, and the fight against colonial censorship.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Key Historical Concepts &amp; Chronology</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>📜 The First Printed Books (East Asia to Europe)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Woodblock printing in China, Japan &amp; Gutenberg's Press</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>China:</strong> From AD 594, books were printed by rubbing paper against inked surface of woodblocks. Accordion book format. China had a vast bureaucratic system that sponsored civil service examination textbooks.</li>
            <li><strong>Japan:</strong> Buddhist missionaries from China introduced hand-printing around AD 768–770. The oldest Japanese book is the Buddhist <em>Diamond Sutra</em> (AD 868).</li>
            <li><strong>Marco Polo:</strong> Returned to Italy from China in 1295 bringing woodblock printing technology.</li>
            <li><strong>Johann Gutenberg (1430s–1448):</strong> In Strasbourg, Germany, Gutenberg invented the mechanical <strong>movable type printing press</strong>. The first book printed was the <strong>Gutenberg Bible</strong> (about 180 copies).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚡ The Print Revolution &amp; Religious Reformation</h3>
          <p class="formula">"Printing is the ultimate gift of God and the greatest one" — Martin Luther</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>A New Reading Public:</strong> Printing reduced the cost of books, time, and labour. Multiple copies could be produced with ease, shifting from an oral hearing culture to a reading culture.</li>
            <li><strong>Protestant Reformation:</strong> In 1517, German religious reformer <strong>Martin Luther</strong> wrote <em>Ninety-Five Theses</em> criticizing practices and rituals of the Roman Catholic Church. Printed copies were posted on church doors and widely read, leading to the division within Christianity.</li>
            <li><strong>Menocchio (16th century):</strong> A miller in Italy read books available in his locality and reinterpreted the message of the Bible; condemned to death by the Roman Catholic Inquisition.</li>
            <li><strong>Index of Prohibited Books (1558):</strong> The Catholic Church imposed severe controls over publishers and booksellers to suppress heretical ideas.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>💡 The Reading Mania &amp; The French Revolution</h3>
          <p class="formula">18th Century Enlightenment &amp; Popular Literature</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Reading Mania:</strong> Literacy rates rose across Europe to 60–80%. Cheap pocket-sized books sold by traveling pedlars: <em>Penny chapbooks</em> in England, <em>Bibliothèque Bleue</em> in France.</li>
            <li>Scientific discoveries of Isaac Newton and philosophical writings of <strong>Voltaire</strong> and <strong>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</strong> were mass-published, popularizing reason, rationality, and critical thinking.</li>
            <li><strong>Print and the French Revolution:</strong>
              <br/>1. Print popularized the ideas of Enlightenment thinkers who criticized tradition, superstition, and despotism.
              <br/>2. Created a new culture of dialogue, public debate, and questioning authority.
              <br/>3. Cartoons and caricatures attacked the monarchy's lavish morality while common people suffered.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🇮🇳 India and the World of Print &amp; Censorship</h3>
          <p class="formula">From Palm Leaf Manuscripts to Vernacular Press Act (1878)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>First Press in India:</strong> Brought to Goa by Portuguese Jesuit priests in the mid-16th century. First printed Tamil book in Cochin (1579). English printing started in 1780 with James Augustus Hickey's <em>Bengal Gazette</em>.</li>
            <li><strong>Social and Religious Debates:</strong> Raja Rammohan Roy published <em>Sambad Kaumudi</em> (1821) attacking sati; orthodox Hindus published <em>Samachar Chandrika</em>. Urdu lithographic presses like Deoband Seminary (1866) published fatwas.</li>
            <li><strong>Women Writers:</strong> Rashsundari Debi secretly learned to read and wrote <em>Amar Jiban</em> (1876, first full autobiography in Bengali); Tarabai Shinde and Pandita Ramabai highlighted upper-caste widows' oppression.</li>
            <li><strong>Vernacular Press Act of 1878:</strong> Modeled on the Irish Press Laws, passed by Lord Lytton to censor vernacular newspapers criticizing colonial policies. Bal Gangadhar Tilak was arrested in 1908 for writing in his Marathi paper <em>Kesari</em>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Printing Technology Evolution &amp; Social Impact</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · Evolution of Printing Technology</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Timeline of printing technology">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Timeline Steps -->
              <!-- Step 1: Woodblock AD 594 -->
              <rect x="15" y="30" width="130" height="75" rx="6" fill="#f1f5f9" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="80" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#334155">AD 594 · China</text>
              <text x="80" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#475569">Woodblock Printing</text>
              <text x="80" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#64748b">Hand rubbing on paper</text>
              <text x="80" y="97" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#64748b">Accordion format</text>

              <!-- Arrow 1 -->
              <line x1="145" y1="67" x2="165" y2="67" stroke="#0f766e" stroke-width="2"/>

              <!-- Step 2: Gutenberg 1448 -->
              <rect x="165" y="30" width="130" height="75" rx="6" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="230" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">1448 · Gutenberg</text>
              <text x="230" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#075985">Movable Type Press</text>
              <text x="230" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#0284c7">Metal letter moulds</text>
              <text x="230" y="97" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#0284c7">Gutenberg Bible</text>

              <!-- Arrow 2 -->
              <line x1="295" y1="67" x2="315" y2="67" stroke="#0f766e" stroke-width="2"/>

              <!-- Step 3: Rotary Press 19th C -->
              <rect x="315" y="30" width="130" height="75" rx="6" fill="#ecfdf5" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="380" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#065f46">19th C · Richard Hoe</text>
              <text x="380" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#047857">Cylindrical Rotary Press</text>
              <text x="380" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#059669">8,000 sheets/hour</text>
              <text x="380" y="97" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#059669">Mass newspaper print</text>

              <!-- Summary Banner -->
              <rect x="25" y="135" width="410" height="90" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#0f766e" stroke-width="2"/>
              <text x="230" y="160" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#0f766e">The Shift from Hearing Public to Reading Public</text>
              <text x="230" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#334155">• Earlier: Books were expensive manuscripts; knowledge was heard at ballads/taverns</text>
              <text x="230" y="198" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#334155">• After Print: Inexpensive paperbacks, illustrated folk tales, universal public literacy</text>
              <text x="230" y="215" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-weight="600" fill="#dc2626">Direct catalyst for the Protestant Reformation, Enlightenment, and National Awakenings</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="visual-caption">Technological transition from East Asian woodblock printing to mechanical presses transformed world literacy.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 2 · Print as an Instrument of Reform &amp; Resistance in India</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Print culture in India impact">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- 4 Sectors of Indian Print -->
              <!-- 1. Social Reform -->
              <rect x="20" y="20" width="195" height="90" rx="6" fill="#eff6ff" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="35" y="42" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#1d4ed8">Social &amp; Religious Reform</text>
              <text x="35" y="60" font-size="9" fill="#1e3a8a">• Raja Rammohan Roy: Sambad Kaumudi</text>
              <text x="35" y="75" font-size="9" fill="#1e3a8a">• Debates on Sati, idolatry, monotheism</text>
              <text x="35" y="90" font-size="9" fill="#1e3a8a">• Vernacular translations of scriptures</text>

              <!-- 2. Women's Voice -->
              <rect x="245" y="20" width="195" height="90" rx="6" fill="#fdf2f8" stroke="#ec4899" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="260" y="42" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#be185d">Women's Emancipation</text>
              <text x="260" y="60" font-size="9" fill="#9d174d">• Rashsundari Debi: Amar Jiban (1876)</text>
              <text x="260" y="75" font-size="9" fill="#9d174d">• Tarabai Shinde: Stri Dharam Vichar</text>
              <text x="260" y="90" font-size="9" fill="#9d174d">• Pandita Ramabai: Plight of widows</text>

              <!-- 3. Caste Reform -->
              <rect x="20" y="125" width="195" height="95" rx="6" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="35" y="147" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#b45309">Caste &amp; Workers' Rights</text>
              <text x="35" y="165" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">• Jyotirao Phule: Gulamgiri (1871)</text>
              <text x="35" y="180" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">• Dr. B.R. Ambedkar &amp; Periyar</text>
              <text x="35" y="195" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">• Millworker Kashibaba: Chhote/Bade</text>

              <!-- 4. Nationalist Resistance -->
              <rect x="245" y="125" width="195" height="95" rx="6" fill="#ecfdf5" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="260" y="147" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#047857">Nationalist Resistance</text>
              <text x="260" y="165" font-size="9" fill="#065f46">• Bal Gangadhar Tilak: Kesari (Marathi)</text>
              <text x="260" y="180" font-size="9" fill="#065f46">• Exposed colonial exploitation</text>
              <text x="260" y="195" font-size="9" fill="#065f46">• Defied Vernacular Press Act 1878</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="visual-caption">Print gave marginalized groups—women, lower castes, and nationalists—a voice to challenge established social and colonial hierarchies.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Interactive 20-Question Quiz -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Interactive CBSE Board Exam Quiz (20 Questions)</h2>
      <p class="muted">Test your concepts on print history, Gutenberg's revolution, and Indian vernacular presses with board exam standard MCQs.</p>

      <div class="quiz-container card" id="quiz-box">
        <div class="quiz-header-bar">
          <span class="quiz-counter" id="quiz-counter">Question 1 of 20</span>
          <span class="quiz-score" id="quiz-score">Score: 0 / 20</span>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-question-body">
          <p class="quiz-q-text" id="quiz-question">Loading question...</p>
          <div class="quiz-options-list" id="quiz-options"></div>
          <div class="quiz-feedback" id="quiz-feedback"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
          <button class="button secondary" id="quiz-prev-btn" disabled>← Previous</button>
          <button class="button" id="quiz-next-btn" disabled>Next Question →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-completed-banner card" id="quiz-result-box" style="display:none; text-align:center; padding:2.5rem;">
        <h3>Quiz Completed! 🎉</h3>
        <p class="quiz-final-score" id="quiz-final-score" style="font-size:1.4rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--brand); margin:1rem 0;"></p>
        <p id="quiz-final-msg" class="muted"></p>
        <button class="button" id="quiz-restart-btn" style="margin-top:1rem;">Retake Quiz</button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Need personalized guidance for Class 10 Social Science?</h2>
      <p>Book a free {provider.trial.duration_minutes}-minute trial session with {provider.tutor_name} (online or at-home) to master answer-writing frameworks and score 100% in CBSE Board Exams.</p>
      <a class="button" href={calBookingUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Book Free Trial</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
(function() {
  const quizData = [
    {
      q: "Which was the oldest dated printed book of Japan?",
      options: ["The Bible", "The Diamond Sutra", "The Tale of Genji", "Ukiyo"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The oldest printed book in Japan is the Buddhist Diamond Sutra, printed in AD 868, containing six sheets of text and woodcut illustrations."
    },
    {
      q: "Who brought the knowledge of woodblock printing back to Italy from China in 1295?",
      options: ["Marco Polo", "Christopher Columbus", "Vasco da Gama", "Johann Gutenberg"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "In 1295, Marco Polo, a great explorer, returned to Italy after many years of exploration in China and brought back the technology of woodblock printing."
    },
    {
      q: "Who invented the mechanical printing press with movable metal types in the 1430s?",
      options: ["Martin Luther", "Erasmus", "Johann Gutenberg", "William Caxton"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "Johann Gutenberg developed the first known mechanical printing press in Strasbourg, Germany, perfecting the movable type system by 1448."
    },
    {
      q: "What was the first book printed by Johann Gutenberg on his printing press?",
      options: ["The Quran", "The Gutenberg Bible", "Ninety-Five Theses", "Dictionary"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The first book Gutenberg printed was the Bible. About 180 copies were printed and it took three years to produce them."
    },
    {
      q: "Martin Luther's 'Ninety-Five Theses' (1517) led to which major religious movement?",
      options: ["The Counter Reformation", "The Protestant Reformation", "The Crusades", "The Renaissance"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Martin Luther wrote Ninety-Five Theses criticizing many practices of the Roman Catholic Church, which sparked the Protestant Reformation."
    },
    {
      q: "Who was Menocchio and why was he executed by the Roman Catholic Church?",
      options: ["A printer who printed anti-clerical cartoons", "An Italian miller who reinterpreted the Bible, prompting the Inquisition", "A translator who translated Bible into English", "A monk who supported Martin Luther"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Menocchio, a miller in northern Italy, reinterpreted the message of the Bible and formulated a view of God and Creation that enraged the Catholic Church, resulting in his execution."
    },
    {
      q: "Why did the Roman Catholic Church publish the 'Index of Prohibited Books' starting in 1558?",
      options: ["To ban scientific textbooks", "To control and censor books spreading heretical ideas questioning Church authority", "To promote Latin books only", "To prevent translation of the Bible into Italian"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Troubled by the effects of popular readings and questionings of faith, the Roman Catholic Church imposed control over publishers and created the Index of Prohibited Books in 1558."
    },
    {
      q: "What were 'Penny chapbooks' sold in England during the reading mania?",
      options: ["Expensive leather-bound encyclopedias", "Pocket-sized cheap booklets sold by traveling pedlars called chapmen for a penny", "Official royal decrees", "Secret revolutionary pamphlets"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Penny chapbooks were small, pocket-sized booklets sold by traveling pedlars (chapmen) for a single penny, making them affordable to ordinary working people."
    },
    {
      q: "Which French thinker wrote: 'Tremble, therefore, tyrants of the world! Tremble before the virtual writer!'?",
      options: ["Voltaire", "Jean-Jacques Rousseau", "Louis-Sébastien Mercier", "Montesquieu"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "Louis-Sébastien Mercier, an 18th-century French novelist, declared that the printing press was the most powerful engine of progress, warning despots to tremble."
    },
    {
      q: "Where was the first printing press established in India, and by whom?",
      options: ["In Goa by Portuguese Jesuit priests in the mid-16th century", "In Calcutta by the British East India Company in 1780", "In Bombay by Parsi merchants in 1820", "In Madras by Dutch traders in 1650"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "The printing press first came to Goa with Portuguese Jesuit missionaries in the mid-sixteenth century."
    },
    {
      q: "Who published the weekly magazine 'Bengal Gazette' in 1780, described as 'a commercial paper open to all, but influenced by none'?",
      options: ["James Augustus Hickey", "Warren Hastings", "William Jones", "Raja Rammohan Roy"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "James Augustus Hickey began editing the Bengal Gazette in 1780, publishing private advertisements and gossip about senior Company officials."
    },
    {
      q: "Which newspaper was started by Raja Rammohan Roy in 1821 to campaign against practices like Sati?",
      options: ["Samachar Chandrika", "Sambad Kaumudi", "Mirat-ul-Akhbar", "Kesari"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Raja Rammohan Roy published Sambad Kaumudi from 1821 to propagate his reformist ideas, while orthodox Hindus founded Samachar Chandrika to oppose him."
    },
    {
      q: "Who wrote 'Amar Jiban' (1876), the first full-length autobiography published in the Bengali language?",
      options: ["Kailashbashini Debi", "Rashsundari Debi", "Tarabai Shinde", "Pandita Ramabai"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Rashsundari Debi, an orthodox housewife in East Bengal, secretly taught herself to read in kitchen candlelight and wrote 'Amar Jiban' in 1876."
    },
    {
      q: "Which social reformer wrote 'Gulamgiri' (1871) dedicated to the American movement against slavery?",
      options: ["Dr. B.R. Ambedkar", "Jyotirao Phule", "Periyar E.V. Ramasamy", "Narayana Guru"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "In 1871, Jyotirao Phule wrote Gulamgiri (Slavery), linking the plight of Indian lower castes with the struggles of Black slaves in America."
    },
    {
      q: "The Vernacular Press Act was passed by the British colonial government in:",
      options: ["1857", "1878", "1885", "1905"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "In 1878, the Vernacular Press Act was passed by Lord Lytton, modeled on the Irish Press Laws, allowing the government to seize printing presses of critical vernacular newspapers."
    },
    {
      q: "Which nationalist leader was arrested in 1908 for writing sympathetic articles about revolutionaries in his Marathi newspaper 'Kesari'?",
      options: ["Bal Gangadhar Tilak", "Lala Lajpat Rai", "Bipin Chandra Pal", "Gopal Krishna Gokhale"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "Bal Gangadhar Tilak wrote with great sympathy about deported Punjabi revolutionaries in his Marathi paper Kesari, leading to his imprisonment in 1908 and widespread protests."
    },
    {
      q: "Who published 'Stri Dharma Vichar' and wrote passionately about women's experiences in Maharashtra?",
      options: ["Tarabai Shinde", "Kailashbashini Debi", "Begum Rokeya", "Sarojini Naidu"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "Tarabai Shinde and Pandita Ramabai wrote furiously about the miserable condition of women, especially upper-caste widows, in late 19th-century Maharashtra."
    },
    {
      q: "Which book was written by Kanpur millworker Kashibaba in 1938 to expose caste and class exploitation?",
      options: ["Gulamgiri", "Chhote Aur Bade Ka Sawal", "Sacchi Kavitayan", "Anandamath"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Kashibaba, a Kanpur millworker, wrote and published Chhote Aur Bade Ka Sawal in 1938 to demonstrate the links between caste and class exploitation."
    },
    {
      q: "What were 'Biliotheque Bleue' in France?",
      options: ["Royal law books", "Low-priced small books printed on poor quality paper and bound in cheap blue covers", "Books of scientific discoveries", "Religious bibles for churches"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "In France, 'Bibliothèque Bleue' were cheap pocket-sized books printed on poor quality paper and bound in cheap blue covers, sold to common readers."
    },
    {
      q: "How did print culture help create conditions that led to the French Revolution?",
      options: ["By popularizing Enlightenment ideas that exposed the irrationality of despotism", "By creating a new culture of open public dialogue and debate", "By ridiculing the monarchy through political cartoons and caricatures", "All of the above"],
      answer: 3,
      explanation: "Historians argue that print popularized Enlightenment writings (Voltaire, Rousseau), fostered public debate, and exposed royal corruption through cartoons—all contributing to the revolutionary atmosphere."
    }
  ];

  let currentIdx = 0;
  let userAnswers = new Array(quizData.length).fill(null);

  const qText = document.getElementById("quiz-question");
  const optionsBox = document.getElementById("quiz-options");
  const feedbackBox = document.getElementById("quiz-feedback");
  const counterBox = document.getElementById("quiz-counter");
  const scoreBox = document.getElementById("quiz-score");
  const prevBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-prev-btn");
  const nextBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-next-btn");
  const quizBox = document.getElementById("quiz-box");
  const resultBox = document.getElementById("quiz-result-box");
  const finalScore = document.getElementById("quiz-final-score");
  const finalMsg = document.getElementById("quiz-final-msg");
  const restartBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-restart-btn");

  function renderQuestion(idx) {
    const item = quizData[idx];
    counterBox.textContent = `Question ${idx + 1} of ${quizData.length}`;
    qText.textContent = item.q;
    optionsBox.innerHTML = "";
    feedbackBox.textContent = "";
    feedbackBox.className = "quiz-feedback";

    const selected = userAnswers[idx];

    item.options.forEach((optText, oIdx) => {
      const btn = document.createElement("button");
      btn.className = "quiz-opt-btn";
      btn.textContent = `${String.fromCharCode(65 + oIdx)}. ${optText}`;

      if (selected !== null) {
        btn.disabled = true;
        if (oIdx === item.answer) {
          btn.classList.add("opt-correct");
        } else if (oIdx === selected) {
          btn.classList.add("opt-wrong");
        }
      } else {
        btn.onclick = () => selectOption(oIdx);
      }

      optionsBox.appendChild(btn);
    });

    if (selected !== null) {
      if (selected === item.answer) {
        feedbackBox.textContent = `✓ Correct! ${item.explanation}`;
        feedbackBox.classList.add("feedback-correct");
      } else {
        feedbackBox.textContent = `✗ Incorrect. Correct answer is option ${String.fromCharCode(65 + item.answer)}. ${item.explanation}`;
        feedbackBox.classList.add("feedback-wrong");
      }
    }

    prevBtn.disabled = idx === 0;
    nextBtn.disabled = selected === null;
    nextBtn.textContent = idx === quizData.length - 1 ? "Finish Quiz →" : "Next Question →";
    updateScoreDisplay();
  }

  function selectOption(oIdx) {
    userAnswers[currentIdx] = oIdx;
    renderQuestion(currentIdx);
  }

  function updateScoreDisplay() {
    let score = 0;
    userAnswers.forEach((ans, i) => {
      if (ans === quizData[i].answer) score++;
    });
    scoreBox.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${quizData.length}`;
  }

  prevBtn.onclick = () => {
    if (currentIdx > 0) {
      currentIdx--;
      renderQuestion(currentIdx);
    }
  };

  nextBtn.onclick = () => {
    if (currentIdx < quizData.length - 1) {
      currentIdx++;
      renderQuestion(currentIdx);
    } else {
      finishQuiz();
    }
  };

  function finishQuiz() {
    quizBox.style.display = "none";
    resultBox.style.display = "block";
    let score = 0;
    userAnswers.forEach((ans, i) => {
      if (ans === quizData[i].answer) score++;
    });
    finalScore.textContent = `Your Final Score: ${score} / ${quizData.length} (${Math.round((score / quizData.length) * 100)}%)`;
    if (score >= 18) {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Outstanding mastery! You have exceptional clarity on Print Culture in Europe and India.";
    } else if (score >= 14) {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Great job! Review Indian women writers and the Vernacular Press Act of 1878 to score 100%.";
    } else {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Review the Gutenberg press, reading revolution, and Indian print reform above, then retry!";
    }
  }

  restartBtn.onclick = () => {
    userAnswers = new Array(quizData.length).fill(null);
    currentIdx = 0;
    resultBox.style.display = "none";
    quizBox.style.display = "block";
    renderQuestion(0);
  };

  renderQuestion(0);
})();
</script>

